WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.940
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.920
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.980
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.500
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.680
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.180
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.580
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.560
Today, we look at some of the best
research on plant based meat.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.560
And we start with
the SWAP-MEAT study

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.066
that put Beyond Meat
products to the test.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.360
As the chair of Harvard’s
Nutrition department put it,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.660
transitioning global diets
towards healthy plant-based

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.960
dietary patterns would require
large-scale public health efforts

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.980
but could be instrumental
in ensuring future human health.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.240
In my book How Not to Diet,
in the chapter on habit formation,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.320
I talk about how you can change
your existing bad habits to good ones,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.420
or establish new good
habits from scratch,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.600
using a technique known
as implementation intentions.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.900
Instead of vague self-promises
to “do our best,”

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.920
implementation intentions
are specific if-then plans

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.040
to perform a particular
behavior in a specific context.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.700
They take the form
of When situation X arises,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.500
I will perform response Y.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.940
For example, If I get hungry
after dinner, I will eat an apple.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.540
If the triggering circumstance
is a regular, daily occurrence,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.040
implementation intentions can be
the beginning of a beautiful habit.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.000
So, what about using Implementation
Intentions to cut down on meat?

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.020
After all, most food choices
are just due to ingrained habits,

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.460
and meat is no exception.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.500
Here, they were
trying to get people

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.660
to change from
a climate perspective,

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.600
noting more greenhouse gas
emissions from animal ag

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.260
than all cars, trucks, trains,
ships, and planes combined,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.440
but whatever the reason,
researchers randomized people

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.860
to form “if–then plans”
(for example, “If I visit

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.720
the university cafeteria
for lunch tomorrow,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.420
then I will choose one
of the vegetarian meals”).

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.500
Motivating people to consciously
form an implementation intention

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.360
specifically how to implement a goal
seems to be such a simple technique,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.860
but it works.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.440
Forming an implementation
intention led people

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.720
not only to think more
often about their intention

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.540
to reduce meat consumption
but also to eat less meat

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.240
despite the strong
force of habit.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.460
On one hand, breaking
a habit may be

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.340
one of the most challenging
tasks we set ourselves.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.880
On the other hand,
a first yet effective step

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.940
in getting there seems to be
writing down the goal of change

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.820
in the form of an if-then
statement on a piece of paper.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.860
Even a journey of a thousand
miles begins with a single step.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.220
How else can we transition
to reduced-meat diets?

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.400
We talked about making the healthier
option the default option.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.640
We could also minimize disruption,
by producing affordable,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.640
recognizable, and tasty
plant-based meat alternatives.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.100
The topic of plant-based
meat alternatives

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.380
has been discussed
for several decades,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.020
but it has only recently become
one of the hottest topics

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.460
in the food
and research communities.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.400
After all, one of the largest
perceived barriers

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.000
for switching diets was the
enjoyment of eating meat, and so,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.100
if you can deliver a similar
taste and mouthfeel,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.600
it could help people shift.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.000
And it seems
like it’s working.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.881
In recent years in North America,
plant-meat sales grew by 37 percent.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.560
The company that makes
the Beyond Burger

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.840
decided to put up
or shut up

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.180
by funding a study
through Chris Gardner’s

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.560
prestigious lab at Stanford.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.500
A randomized crossover
trial to compare the effect

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.160
of consuming plant-based
alternative meat to animal meat

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.280
on human health by having people
eat at least 2 servings a day

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.100
of Plant versus Animal
meat for 8 weeks each,

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.680
while keeping everything
else as similar as possible.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.460
So, instead of burgers and beef
from cows, sausage from pigs,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.340
and breasts from chickens,
they ate burgers, sausage,

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.020
and chicken made from plants.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.800
Now, if they were trying to game
it to give Beyond Meat an advantage,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.500
they’d have chosen the worst
possible meats to compare it to,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.380
but no, the meats
were organic, grass-fed

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.900
to give the fairest
comparison possible.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.920
So, what happened? Significant
drop in TMAO levels

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.720
during the plant-
based meat phase.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.760
That’s a good thing,
as I’ve explored before.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.940
The consumption of meat dairy
and eggs can be turned

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.280
by bad gut bacteria
into trimethylamine,

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.980
which is oxidized by
our liver into TMAO,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.460
which is associated with
heart failure, kidney failure,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.460
and our number one killer,
atherosclerosis,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.620
the hardening of our arteries.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.600
The plant-based meat
group also achieved

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.160
a significant drop in cholesterol.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.100
No surprise, given the lower
saturated fat intake.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.120
Of course, if they had been
eating whole plant foods instead,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.000
like beans, saturated fat
would have been way down,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.580
fiber way up,
and sodium way down.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.940
In fact, there was basically
no difference in sodium intake;

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.460
so, no surprise,
no difference in blood pressure.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.800
The big surprise was weight.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.480
During the plant-
based meat phase,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.520
they inadvertently
lost a couple pounds.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.980
Some of the plant-based
meats are highly processed,

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.200
and normally you’d expect
people to gain weight;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.920
however, weight was modestly but
statistically significantly lower

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.940
after 8 weeks on the Plant
rather than on the Animal phase.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.120
Notably, this was observed
despite no differences

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.280
in reported total
calorie intake

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.560
or physical activity
levels between each phase.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.140
Same calories,
yet less weight.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.940
We’ve seen this before.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.000
Same calorie restriction,
yet more weight-loss

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.940
eating more plant-based,
slimmer waist, less body fat.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.360
This may be from the fewer
branched-chain amino acids

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.520
in plant protein
compared to animal protein

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.240
or because the resting metabolic
rate in those eating vegetarian

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.400
is as much as
20 percent higher;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.911
so, you’re basically just burning
more calories in your sleep.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.220
Finally today,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.407
we look at the different impacts
of plant vs animal protein.

00:05:48.812 --> 00:05:52.260
So, are these plant-based
burgers healthy or not?

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.400
And the answer is…
compared to what?

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.920
Eating is kind of a zero-sum game;
every food has an opportunity cost.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.600
I mean, every time we
put something in our mouth

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.020
it’s a lost opportunity to put
something even healthier in our mouth.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.640
So, if you want to know
if something is healthy,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.180
you have to compare it to
what you’d be eating instead.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.460
So, for example,
are eggs healthy?

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.820
Compared to a breakfast
link sausage? Yes!

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.460
But compared to oatmeal?
Not even close.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.140
But look, sausage is considered
a group 1 carcinogen.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.280
In other words, we know consumption
of processed meat causes cancer.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.560
Each 50-gram serving a day,
that’s a single breakfast link,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.160
was linked to an 18% higher
risk of colorectal cancer.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.000
So, the risk of getting colorectal
cancer eating one link a day

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.620
is about the same as the increased
risk of lung cancer you’d get

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.760
breathing secondhand smoke all
day living with a smoking spouse.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.420
So, compared to sausage,
eggs are healthy,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.000
but compared to oatmeal,
eggs are not.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.940
So, when it comes to Beyond Meat
and Impossible Burger,

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.240
yeah, they may be better in
that they have less saturated fat,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.700
but, hey, you want
less saturated fat?

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.740
Plant-based meat
alternatives are no match

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.890
for unprocessed plant foods,
such as beans or lentils.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.800
And a bean burrito or lentil
soup could certainly fill

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.620
the same culinary niche
as a lunchtime burger.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.620
But if you are going to
have some kind of burger,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.220
it’s easy to argue that the
plant-based versions are healthier.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.180
There is a sodium issue, and
it’s not that much, if any, lower,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.160
in saturated fat, since
they use coconut oil,

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.900
which is basically just
as bad as animal fat;

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.440
there’s not much
advantage on that front.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.200
Though the total protein is
similar across the board,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.240
does this matter? Or Is there any
advantage to eating plant protein

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.540
over animal protein?
Let’s look at the association

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.960
between animal and plant
protein intake and mortality.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.440
In the twin Harvard cohorts,
following more than 100,000 men

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.560
and women over decades, “…after
adjusting for other dietary

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.960
and lifestyle factors, animal
protein intake was associated

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.100
with a higher risk [of] mortality,
particularly [dying from

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.520
cardiovascular disease], whereas
higher plant protein intake

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.980
was associated with
a] lower all-cause mortality”,

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.820
meaning a lower risk of dying
from all causes put together.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.880
So, “replacing animal protein
of various origins

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.700
with plant protein was associated
with lower mortality”,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.480
especially if you’re replacing
processed meat and egg protein,

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:25.320
which were the worst. But when
it comes to living a longer life,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.760
plant protein sources beat out
each and every animal protein source.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.560
Not just better
than bacon and eggs,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.280
but better than burgers, chicken,
turkey, fish, and dairy protein.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.160
Together with other studies, these
“findings support the importance

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.980
of protein sources for the
long-term health outcome

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.760
and suggest plants constitute
a preferred protein source

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.460
compared [to] animal foods.” Why?

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.000
Well, unlike animal protein, plant
protein has not been associated

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.100
with increased levels
of the cancer-promoting

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.660
growth hormone IGF-1, for example.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.840
Now, soy protein is similar
enough to animal protein

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.380
that at high enough doses, like eating
two Impossible Burgers a day,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.000
you may bump your IGF-1.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.360
But the only reason we care
about IGF-1 is cancer risk,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.940
and if anything, higher soy
intake is associated with

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.040
a decreased risk of cancer.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.700
For example, a recent systematic
review and meta-analysis found

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.460
that soy protein intake was
associated with a decreased risk

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.240
in breast cancer mortality;
we’re talking “a 12 percent reduction

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.840
in breast cancer death
[associated with] each 5-gram-a-day

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.640
increase in soy protein intake.”

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.940
But the high soy groups
in these studies

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.760
were on the order of
more than 16 grams a day,

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.460
associated with a
whopping 62% lower risk

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:36.300
of dying from breast cancer.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.660
More than 10 grams of soy
protein a day may be good,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.780
associated with cutting
breast cancer mortality risk

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.480
nearly in half, and getting
more than 16 grams a day

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.660
may be better, which is like
one Impossible Burger a day.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.580
But we simply don’t
know what happens

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:52.800
at consumption levels
far above that.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.760
Plant protein has also been
linked to lower blood pressure,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.180
reduced LDL cholesterol, and
improved insulin sensitivity.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.480
No wonder “substitution of
plant protein for animal protein

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.980
has been related to a lower incidence

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.920
of cardiovascular disease
and type 2 diabetes.”

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.780
Indeed, 21 different studies following
nearly a half million people,

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.420
and “high… animal protein
intakes [were] associated with

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:16.920
an increased risk of [type 2 diabetes],

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.680
whereas [even just] moderate
plant protein intake

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.460
is associated with a decreased
risk of [type 2 diabetes].”

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.700
OK, but these were just
observational studies.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.700
They all tried to control for other
dietary and lifestyle factors,

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:35.760
but you can’t prove cause-and-effect,
until...you put it to the test.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.240
The “Effect of Replacing Animal
Protein with Plant Protein

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.600
on [blood sugar] Control in
Diabetes: A Systematic Review

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.180
and Meta-Analysis of
Randomized Controlled Trials.”

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:48.120
Even just switching out about
a third of your protein

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.840
from animal to plant sources
yielded significant improvements

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:55.080
in long-term blood sugar control,
and fasting blood sugars, and insulin.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.120
You can do the same thing
looking at cholesterol.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.300
Here’s a systematic review and
meta-analysis of randomized

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.820
controlled trials on the effect
of plant protein on blood fats.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.240
And indeed, swapping in plant
protein for animal protein

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.420
decreases LDL cholesterol,
and this benefit occurs

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.360
whether you start out at high
cholesterol or low cholesterol,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.300
whether you’re swapping out
dairy, or meat, and eggs,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.500
and whether you’re swapping in
soy or other plant proteins.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.220
We’ve known about the beneficial
effects of soy on cholesterol

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.240
going back nearly 40 years, but
other sources of plant protein

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.520
can do it as well. Yeah, but
we’re not swapping beans for beef.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.240
These products are mostly
just isolated plant proteins,

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.720
mostly pea protein isolate
in the case of Beyond,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.360
and concentrated soy protein
in the case of Impossible.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.300
If you just isolate out
the plant proteins themselves

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.280
are you still going to get benefits?

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.880
Yes, surprisingly. Check it out.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.300
Interestingly, the researchers
concluded, that they did not ﬁnd

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.420
a signiﬁcant difference between
protein isolate products

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.720
and whole food sources, “suggesting
that the cholesterol-lowering

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.640
effects are at least,
in part, attributable

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.340
to the plant protein
itself rather than just

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:06.540
the associated nutrients.”

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:08.940
So, it’s not just because
plant protein travels

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.160
with fiber or less saturated fat.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.300
Plant proteins break down
into a different distribution

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.420
of amino acids; and so, it’s
like if you give people arginine,

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.580
an amino acid found
more in plant foods,

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.980
that alone can bring
down people’s cholesterol.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:26.700
And even plant protein concentrates
used in these products

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.820
aren’t pure protein, retaining
a few active compounds

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:35.805
such as phytosterols and antioxidants,
which also can have beneficial effects.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.180
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.940
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.820
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:48.360
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:54.420
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.060
go to the NutritionFacts
Podcast landing page.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.250
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:03.120
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.900
My last two books are
“How to Survive a Pandemic”

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.000
and my “How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.300
Get ready this year for the
launch of How Not to Age.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.760
And, of course, all the proceeds
I receive from the sales

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.100
of all my books goes
directly to charity.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.880
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.620
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.580
on the latest in nutrition research
with bite-sized videos and articles

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:28.800
uploaded nearly every day.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.140
Everything on the
website is free.

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.540
There’s no ads, no
corporate sponsorships,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.320
no kickbacks.

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.480
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.080
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:42.300
as a tribute to
my grandmother,

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:46.550
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

